<div>
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="mb-12">
        <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm mb-4">
            <a href="/docs/development-tools/linting" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Development Tools</a>
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-gray-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-white">Code Graph</span>
        </nav>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Code Graph</span>
            <span class="text-gray-500 text-2xl font-medium">Graph RAG for agents</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            <code>soli graph build</code> extracts a graph of your project's source &mdash; files, classes,
            models, controllers, methods, functions, routes, views and the database collections they use, plus
            the relationships between them &mdash; and stores it in SolidB. Every node's text is embedded, so AI agents (and your own tools) can retrieve
            the right code by <strong class="text-white">semantic search</strong> and then
            <strong class="text-white">traverse relationships</strong> from there. It's a graph RAG index of your
            own codebase. It also keeps what agents learn: <code>soli graph note</code> anchors a finding to the
            code, and every query is logged, so later queries
            <a href="#section-learn" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">return both</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20 mb-10">
        <p class="text-amber-200 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-white">SoliDB only.</strong> The graph store, its traversals, and the
            embeddings behind semantic retrieval are SoliDB engine features, so
            <code>soli graph build</code> needs a SoliDB connection. The SQL adapters
            (<code>postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>, <code>sqlite</code>) do not implement graph or
            vector storage &mdash; see
            <a href="/docs/database/multi-database#sql" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">SQL document backends</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Building -->
    <section id="section-build" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Building the graph</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Where <code>Model.rag</code> indexes your <em>application data</em>, this points the same vector +
            graph machinery at the <em>code itself</em>.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm"># Build the graph for the app in the current folder
soli graph build

# Or point at a project folder
soli graph build path/to/app</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The build is <strong class="text-white">incremental and non-destructive</strong>. It hashes every
            source file (MD5), so a re-run when nothing changed is a fast no-op
            (<code>✓ code graph already up to date</code>). When something changed, it updates SolidB
            <strong class="text-white">in place</strong> &mdash; inserts new nodes/edges, updates changed ones,
            prunes removed ones &mdash; rather than dropping the collections, so a concurrent reader never sees an
            empty graph and <strong class="text-white">unchanged embeddings are reused</strong> (only changed text
            is re-embedded). Pass <code>--fresh</code> for a full clean rebuild. A progress bar tracks the parse
            &rarr; embed &rarr; sync phases so a large project doesn't look frozen.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Keeping it fresh automatically (dev)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Run the dev server with an embedding key configured (<code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_API_KEY</code>) and the
            graph reindexes itself whenever you save a <code>.sl</code>/<code>.slv</code> file &mdash; no flag
            needed, it just stays current while you work.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli serve . --dev   # auto-reindex on (embedding key present)</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            It rides the dev file-watcher (debounced), runs on a background thread (off the request path), and
            reuses the live route table instead of re-executing <code>routes.sl</code>. It's
            <strong class="text-white">incremental on the expensive part</strong>: nodes whose text is unchanged
            keep their existing embedding and only what changed is re-embedded, so a one-file save costs a
            re-parse and a handful of embeddings &mdash; not thousands &mdash; and the semantic layer stays intact.
            Set <code>SOLI_GRAPH_WATCH=0</code> to disable, or <code>=1</code> to force it on even without a key.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Configuration -->
    <section id="section-config" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Configuration &amp; flags</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            By default every node is embedded (vector index included). Configure the embedding provider with the
            standard variables (OpenAI by default); the graph connects to the
            <strong class="text-white">same SolidB the app's Models use</strong>
            (<code>SOLIDB_HOST</code>, <code>SOLIDB_DATABASE</code>, credentials), loaded from your
            <code>.env</code> just like <code>soli db:seed</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl ring-1 ring-white/10 mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">Variable</th><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">Default</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_API_KEY</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><em>required to embed</em></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_URL</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>.../v1/embeddings</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>text-embedding-3-small</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_TIMEOUT_SECS</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>60</code> &mdash; per-request timeout; bounds a slow/unreachable endpoint</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="my-6 p-5 rounded-xl bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/20">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-sky-300 mb-2"><code>soli graph build</code> appears to hang?</h4>
            <p class="text-sky-200/80 text-sm">
                It embeds by default, so a slow or unreachable <code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_URL</code> is the usual cause.
                Each embedding request times out after <code>SOLI_EMBEDDING_TIMEOUT_SECS</code> (default 60s) and
                fails with a clear message instead of blocking forever. To rule embeddings out entirely, re-run
                with <code>--no-embed</code>.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl ring-1 ring-white/10 mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">Flag</th><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">Effect</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>--no-embed</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">Structural graph only &mdash; skip embeddings and the vector index (fully offline, no API key). Existing embeddings are preserved.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>--database NAME</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">Write to a specific database instead of <code>SOLIDB_DATABASE</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>--dry-run</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">Print the whole graph as JSON to stdout &mdash; writes nothing to SolidB and calls no embedding API. Great for inspection and CI.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>--fresh</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">Force a full clean rebuild (drop + recreate) instead of the default incremental sync &mdash; e.g. after changing the embedding model.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm"># Inspect what would be built, without a database or API key
soli graph build --dry-run | jq '.nodes | length'</code></pre>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Any codebase -->
    <section id="section-any-codebase" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Any codebase (multi-language)</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code>soli graph</code> isn't limited to Soli apps &mdash; point it at <strong class="text-white">any
            repository</strong> and pick which files to index. Storage, embeddings, incremental sync and
            <code>soli graph query</code> are identical; only the extractor changes. SolidB settings come from the
            project's <code>.env</code>, just like a Soli app &mdash; a non-Soli repo won't have these, so add them:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename=".env"><code class="language-bash text-sm">SOLIDB_HOST=http://localhost:6745    # required
SOLIDB_DATABASE=myapp_codegraph      # required (any name; created on first write)
SOLIDB_USERNAME=admin                # required for auth …
SOLIDB_PASSWORD=secret               # … (or SOLIDB_JWT / SOLIDB_API_KEY)

# optional — only to embed for semantic search; else use --no-embed
SOLI_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=sk-...</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm"># index a Rails app: Ruby + templates
soli graph build /path/to/rails-app --ext rb,erb,slim

# or commit a .soligraph.toml and just run:
soli graph build</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Structural extraction (tree-sitter):</strong> Ruby, Python, JavaScript/JSX,
            TypeScript/TSX, Rust and C# get real <code>class</code>/<code>module</code>/<code>method</code>/<code>function</code>
            nodes plus <code>inherits</code>, <code>implements</code> and <code>imports</code> edges; an import lands on the
            project file it names (Python&rsquo;s <code>a.b.c</code> and <code>..b.c</code>, packages&rsquo; <code>__init__.py</code>,
            relative <code>require</code>s and JS/TS imports), on an <code>external</code> stub otherwise.
            <strong class="text-white">Python gets a call graph</strong>: <code>self.m()</code> / <code>cls.m()</code> through the
            class's bases, <code>super().m()</code>, <code>Class.m()</code>, <code>x.m()</code> when <code>x</code>'s class is certain
            (a typed parameter, <code>x = Class(&hellip;)</code>, <code>x = Registry.get(Class)</code>), a bare call to the one project
            function of that name, and <code>Class(&hellip;)</code> &rarr; <code>instantiates</code>; an ambiguous class name or an
            unknown receiver stays unlinked. <strong class="text-white">C# also gets a call graph</strong>
            &mdash; method-body <code>calls</code> and <code>new</code>&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;<code>instantiates</code> edges, attributed to
            the enclosing method. Every other extension (<code>.erb</code>, <code>.slim</code>, config, &hellip;) is
            <strong class="text-white">chunk-embedded</strong> &mdash; split into windows and embedded &mdash; so semantic
            search still covers it, without structural edges.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename=".soligraph.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">extensions  = ["rb", "erb", "slim"]    # what to index
exclude     = ["spec/", "db/migrate/"] # path substrings to skip
chunk_lines = 50                       # window for chunk-embedded files</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Flags override the file (<code>--ext</code>, <code>--exclude</code>, <code>--config</code>); sensible
            directories are skipped by default (<code>.git</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>vendor</code>,
            <code>tmp</code>, <code>target</code>, dot-dirs, &hellip;).
        </p>
        <div class="my-6 p-5 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-amber-400 mb-2">Call graph</h4>
            <p class="text-amber-200/80 text-sm">
                Cross-language resolution is <strong class="text-amber-200">precision-first</strong>. For C#, method
                bodies are walked for <code>calls</code> and <code>new X()</code> <code>instantiates</code> references;
                an <code>instantiates</code> edge lands only on a <em>project</em> class (framework types like
                <code>new List&lt;T&gt;()</code> are skipped, never stubbed), and a <code>calls</code> edge lands only
                when exactly one project method carries that name &mdash; C#'s heavy overloading means shared names
                (<code>ToString</code>, <code>Add</code>&hellip;) are dropped rather than mis-linked. Treat it as a
                high-confidence subset, not exhaustive; the other foreign languages emit structure only
                (<code>inherits</code>/<code>implements</code>/<code>imports</code>). <code>--fresh</code>,
                <code>--dry-run</code>, <code>--no-embed</code>, incremental MD5 skipping and non-destructive sync all
                work the same as for Soli apps.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Schema -->
    <section id="section-schema" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Schema</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Two SolidB collections hold the graph, namespaced so they never clash with your app data (two more
            keep <a href="#section-learn" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">what agents learn</a>).
            <strong class="text-white">soli_graph_nodes</strong> holds one document per code entity;
            <code>kind</code> is <code>file</code>, <code>class</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>controller</code>,
            <code>method</code>, <code>function</code>, <code>route</code>, <code>view</code>,
            <code>collection</code>, <code>enum</code>, <code>interface</code> or <code>external</code>;
            <code>_key</code> is a sanitized, stable id and the human-readable identity is
            <code>kind</code> + <code>qualified_name</code> (with <code>file</code>, <code>line</code>,
            <code>signature</code>, <code>doc</code>, <code>role</code>, and the embedded <code>text</code>/<code>embedding</code>).
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">soli_graph_edges</strong> holds one document per relationship as a SolidB
            edge (<code>_from</code> / <code>_to</code> reference <code>soli_graph_nodes/&lt;key&gt;</code>):
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl ring-1 ring-white/10 mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold"><code>edge_kind</code></th><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">From &rarr; To</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>defines</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">file &rarr; class/function, class &rarr; method (containment)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>inherits</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">class &rarr; superclass (an <code>external</code> stub for framework bases like <code>Model</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>implements</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">class &rarr; interface</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>imports</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">file &rarr; local file</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>calls</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">method/function &rarr; the function or class-method it calls</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>instantiates</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">method/function &rarr; class it constructs (<code>X.new()</code>, or <code>new X()</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>renders</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">controller action &rarr; view (<code>render</code>/<code>partial</code>); an action that names no view &rarr; its own (<code>posts#index</code> &rarr; <code>posts/index</code>, <code>relation: "implicit"</code>); view &rarr; partial view</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>assigns</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">controller action &rarr; view, one per <code>@x</code> the action sets and the view reads (<code>relation: "@posts"</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>reads</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">method/function &rarr; collection it queries; <code>relation</code> is the operation (<code>where</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>FOR</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>writes</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">method/function &rarr; collection it changes; <code>relation</code> is the operation (<code>create</code>, <code>save</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>REMOVE</code>)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>stored_in</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">model &rarr; its collection (an STI subclass &rarr; its root's)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>redirects</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">method/function &rarr; route whose path matches <code>redirect("/path")</code> (prefers the <code>GET</code> route when a path is served by several verbs)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>routes_to</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">route &rarr; controller action</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>relates</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">model &rarr; model (<code>has_many</code>/<code>belongs_to</code>/<code>edge</code>; DSL name in <code>relation</code>)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="my-6 p-5 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/20">
            <h4 class="text-base font-semibold text-amber-400 mb-2">Precision-first call graph</h4>
            <p class="text-amber-200/80 text-sm mb-3">
                Linked when high-confidence: class-method calls (<code>User.find(...)</code>),
                <code>this.method(...)</code> / <code>@method</code>, a bare <code>method(...)</code> inside a class
                (its own or an inherited method, before a top-level function of that name), bare
                <code>super(...)</code>/<code>super.method(...)</code>, a call written without parentheses
                (<code>Post.count</code>, <code>@save</code>),
                unambiguous bare functions, and instance calls on a <strong>locally typed</strong> variable
                (<code>u = User.new()</code>, <code>let u: User = &hellip;</code>, a parameter annotated with a project class
                (<code>def perform(order: Order)</code>), or
                <code>u = User.find(...)</code> then <code>u.authenticate(...)</code>). Unbound receivers
                (<code>user.save()</code> with no known class) stay unlinked &mdash; an edge is never invented.
                Reassigning a tracked local to a value with no known class drops its type, and a bare
                <code>partial("form")</code> in a view resolves against that view's own directory first.
            </p>
            <pre data-filename="sessions_controller.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">def create(req: Any) -&gt; Any {
  let user = User.find_by("email", req["email"])  # binds user &rarr; User
  user.authenticate(req["password"])              # calls &rarr; User#authenticate
  partial("sessions/form")                        # renders &rarr; sessions/_form
  return redirect("/dashboard")                   # redirects &rarr; matching route
}</code></pre>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Database access -->
    <section id="section-db" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Database access</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Each model gets a <code>collection</code> node, named as the ORM names it (<code>Post</code> &rarr;
            <code>posts</code>, <code>BlogPost</code> &rarr; <code>blog_posts</code>, an STI subclass &rarr; its
            root's), and the code that touches it points there:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li>a model's class methods: <code>Post.where(&hellip;)</code>, <code>Post.find(id)</code>,
                <code>Post.count</code> &rarr; <code>reads</code>; <code>Post.create(&hellip;)</code>,
                <code>Post.update_all(&hellip;)</code>, <code>Post.delete(&hellip;)</code> &rarr; <code>writes</code></li>
            <li>writes on a record: <code>post.save</code>, <code>post.update(&hellip;)</code>,
                <code>post.destroy</code> on a locally typed variable, and <code>@save</code> /
                <code>this.update(&hellip;)</code> inside the model itself</li>
            <li>raw SDBQL in <code>@sdbql{ &hellip; }</code> and in the string given to <code>query(&hellip;)</code> /
                <code>db.query(&hellip;)</code>: <code>FOR x IN posts</code> and a traversal's edge collection
                &rarr; <code>reads</code>; <code>INSERT &hellip; INTO</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> /
                <code>REPLACE</code> / <code>REMOVE</code> / <code>UPSERT &hellip; IN</code> &rarr; <code>writes</code>.
                Names bound by <code>LET</code> or <code>FOR</code> and bind parameters (<code>@@coll</code>) are not
                collections</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            So &ldquo;what writes <code>orders</code>?&rdquo; is one traversal from <code>collection:orders</code>,
            and a query on a method lists the collections it touches, with the operation in each edge's
            <code>relation</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Querying -->
    <section id="section-query" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Querying the graph (for agents)</h2>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">The easy path: <code>soli graph query</code></h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            One command turns a natural-language task into the most relevant code <strong class="text-white">plus
            its immediate relationships</strong> &mdash; the graph-RAG payoff (semantic seed &rarr; graph
            expansion) with no AQL to write. It embeds the question, ANN-searches the vector index for seeds, and
            expands each one hop (callers, callees, routes, views). Built with <code>--no-embed</code> or no
            embedding key? It falls back to a <strong class="text-white">weighted</strong> keyword-ranked scan
            (name/qualified_name beat body text), so it always works.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli graph query "where is authentication handled?"
soli graph query "refund flow" --json --limit 5 --hops 1
soli graph query "invoice validation" --path api/   # scope to one side of a mono-repo
soli graph query "login" --kind method,controller   # only those node kinds</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code>--json</code> emits a structured result an agent parses directly (each seed with
            <code>kind</code>, <code>qualified_name</code>, <code>file</code>/<code>line</code>,
            <code>signature</code>, truncated <code>snippet</code>, <code>score</code>, and a
            <code>neighbors</code> list of <code>{direction, edge_kind, relation?, kind, name, signature?}</code>,
            where <code>relation</code> is the operation of <code>reads</code>/<code>writes</code>, the
            <code>@x</code> of <code>assigns</code> or <code>implicit</code> for an action's own view; plus
            <code>insights</code> and <code>related_queries</code> when there are some, see
            <a href="#section-learn" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">below</a>).
            <code>--limit</code> sets seed count (default 6), <code>--hops</code> the expansion depth
            (default 1). <code>--path PREFIX</code> keeps only seeds whose <code>file</code> starts with
            <code>PREFIX</code>; <code>--kind KINDS</code> keeps only seeds whose <code>kind</code> is in the
            comma-separated list (e.g. <code>method,controller,route</code>); without it, <code>external</code> stubs
            (library modules a file imports) are never results. Neighbours are unaffected and
            ordered with structural edges first (<code>routes_to</code>, <code>calls</code>,
            <code>renders</code>, <code>reads</code>, <code>writes</code>, <code>assigns</code>,
            <code>redirects</code>, &hellip;). Semantic search over-fetches then filters;
            the keyword fallback filters in AQL. The heavy <code>embedding</code> field is never included.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Raw queries</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            For anything the command doesn't cover, agents query SolidB directly. Two moves:
            <strong class="text-white">find</strong> nodes (by name, kind, or semantic similarity), then
            <strong class="text-white">traverse</strong> from them.
        </p>
        <div class="my-6 p-5 rounded-xl bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20">
            <p class="text-blue-200/80 text-sm">
                Traversal starts use <code>soli_graph_nodes/&lt;_key&gt;</code>, not <code>n._id</code>
                (SolidB's <code>_id</code> carries a <code>db:</code> prefix that won't match edge endpoints).
                Build the start with <code>CONCAT("soli_graph_nodes/", n._key)</code>. Traversals support the
                <code>FOR v, e IN &hellip;</code> (vertex + edge) form.
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Find code</h3>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-text text-sm">// All controllers
FOR n IN soli_graph_nodes FILTER n.kind == "controller" RETURN n.name

// A specific route
FOR n IN soli_graph_nodes
  FILTER n.kind == "route" AND n.qualified_name == "sessions#create"
  RETURN n</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Semantic search (needs embeddings) uses the <code>node_vec</code> vector index &mdash; model the
            collection from Soli and call <code>similar</code> / <code>graph_rag</code>:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="app/models/code_node.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class CodeNode < Model
  collection "soli_graph_nodes"
  vector_index "embedding", dimension: 1536, metric: "cosine"
end

# "Where is authentication handled?" -> the most relevant code nodes
let hits = CodeNode.similar("authentication and login", field: "embedding", k: 8)</code></pre>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Traverse relationships</h3>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
            <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-text text-sm">// What handles this route, and what does it reach? (route -> action -> calls/renders)
FOR v, e IN 1..3 OUTBOUND "soli_graph_nodes/route:GET_:login" soli_graph_edges
  RETURN { via: e.edge_kind, kind: v.kind, name: v.qualified_name }

// Which actions render this view? (reverse edges)
FOR v, e IN 1..1 INBOUND "soli_graph_nodes/view:posts:index" soli_graph_edges
  FILTER e.edge_kind == "renders"
  RETURN v.qualified_name

// What does a controller action call?
FOR v, e IN 1..2 OUTBOUND "soli_graph_nodes/method:PostsController.create" soli_graph_edges
  FILTER e.edge_kind IN ["calls", "instantiates"]
  RETURN v.qualified_name</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Semantic <strong class="text-white">seed &rarr; traverse</strong> (graph RAG) combines both:
            <code>similar()</code> finds the relevant nodes, then an OUTBOUND/INBOUND traversal expands to their
            callers, callees, routes and views &mdash; exactly the context an agent needs to make a change.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
            Re-run <code>soli graph build</code> any time to refresh &mdash; it's incremental (hashes files, skips
            when unchanged, updates in place), and in dev it auto-reindexes on save. See the full reference in
            <code>www/docs/graph.md</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What agents learn -->
    <section id="section-learn" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What agents learn: notes and past questions</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The graph says what the code <em>is</em>. What an agent works out about it &mdash; which of three similar
            methods is the real refund path, why a guard exists, where a vague question actually led &mdash; is not
            in the source, and was lost after every session. Two collections keep it beside the graph:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-5 mb-6">
            <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli graph note "Refunds go through Refund#perform; Order#refund is the legacy path, kept for the API" \
  --on Refund#perform,Order#refund --question "how is an order refunded?"
soli graph notes                 # every note, stale ones marked
soli graph forget i3f9c…         # delete one by its key</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code>--on</code> takes the <code>qualified_name</code>s a query prints (<code>PostsController#create</code>,
            <code>Post</code>, <code>posts</code>) or node keys, comma-separated or repeated; a name the graph does not
            hold is refused. The same text on the same anchors updates the note. With an embedding key the note is
            embedded (question first, then the finding) into its own vector index. From then on
            <code>soli graph query</code>:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li>returns the <strong class="text-white">notes</strong> that match the question (semantically, or by
                shared words without an embedding key) and those anchored on one of its results, under
                <code>insights</code>;</li>
            <li>brings a matching note's anchors in as results (up to three, marked <code>"via": "note"</code>), so
                code the search ranked low still comes back;</li>
            <li>lists up to three <strong class="text-white">earlier questions</strong> that share its words, with
                the code each returned, under <code>related_queries</code>. Every query is logged (the last 1,000
                are kept).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A note remembers a fingerprint of each anchor's code (signature, doc and source; not its line, so code
            that only moved does not count). When that code changes, or the node disappears, the note is reported
            <code>"stale": true</code>: it may no longer hold. <code>soli graph build</code>, even
            <code>--fresh</code>, never touches notes or the log &mdash; node keys come from the code's names, so an
            anchor survives every rebuild.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl ring-1 ring-white/10 mb-6">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">Collection</th><th class="py-2.5 px-4 font-semibold">One document per</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>soli_graph_insights</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">note: <code>text</code>, <code>question</code>, <code>anchors</code> (<code>key</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>fingerprint</code>), <code>created_at</code>, <code>embedding</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-2.5 px-4"><code>soli_graph_queries</code></td><td class="py-2.5 px-4">question: <code>question</code>, <code>terms</code>, <code>results</code> (qualified names), <code>at</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400">
            Under <a href="/docs/development-tools/debugging#soli-agent" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline"><code>soli agent</code></a>
            the same is a coding agent's <code>graph_note</code> tool, beside <code>graph</code>.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>
